  "_comment": "The focus tree (docs/LAYERS.md §1; owner-approved 2026-11-06). A focus is a framing of the world: a box of longitude and latitude to fit on the sheet, and the names drawn on it. Nothing here is a border. 'level' is how much of the world the sheet holds (1 the world, 4 a region), not importance. 'parents' may name more than one focus: the same place can be reached through more than one door (Africa from the World and from Afro-Eurasia; Latin America from North and from South America). A box whose east is greater than 180 crosses the antimeridian (east 290 = 70°W). 'label' places the focus's name on its parents' maps (default: the middle of its box). 'stage' or 'page' hand the focus to a curated stage instead of framing the box: the Afro-Eurasia stage, the world stage, the North America page. The North America page's own frames (east, southwest, texas, oregon-country, plains, border-states; added 2026-11-06 for lesson maps, APUSH Units 4–6) carry 'page' too: that page draws its sources re-fitted to the box (us.html?frame=texas). 'projection' may override the automatic choice (src/render/focus.js). Water names are drawn on any focus whose box holds them and whose level is in [min, max]. Region names follow the Afro-Eurasia stage's labels, which follow the AP World framework's World Regions maps (co-teacher question 2).",
